# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
#
# .env.local is gitignored and must NEVER be committed. Vite loads it from the
# project root (see envDir in jsons/vite.config.ts) and inlines VITE_* values into
# the renderer bundle at build time, so the value never lands in git.
#
# On CI the workflow writes .env.local from repository secrets (DEEPSEEK_KEY,
# SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY), so the identical build step works locally and on
# the runner.
#
# NOTE: jsons/.env is read too (vite.config.ts merges both directories). Putting a
# value in only one of the two places used to be silently equivalent to not setting it
# at all, which is how the Supabase URL came to be inlined as `void 0`.

# The DeepSeek API key the app uses as its default (deepseek-reasoner / R1).
#
# Leave this empty to ship an app with no built-in key. The student then pastes her
# own into Settings, where it is stored locally on her machine -- which is the only
# arrangement that puts no credential in the app or the repository at all.
VITE_DEEPSEEK_KEY=

# ── Supabase (Document Vault sync) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED for the cloud Vault. If either value is missing at build time, Vite inlines
# `void 0` and supabase-js throws "supabaseUrl is required." while the renderer bundle
# is still evaluating -- React never mounts and the window is white, with no error
# visible outside the renderer console. src/lib/supabase.ts now degrades to a local
# Vault instead of throwing, and build-mac.yml asserts the packaged bundle contains a
# Supabase host, so this cannot ship silently again.
#
# The anon/publishable key is designed to be public; safety comes from Row Level
# Security on the tables, not from hiding it.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
